e we accept in humble  faith the secrets of nature and the  mysteries of God they attempt to seize  them in haughty arrogance they strike  heaven with their fists now humanism is  of course a very complex phenomenon  Petrarch is a difficult figure to pin  down but i have heard remarks like these  even from students of the humanities  here they have asked about the real  significance of the study of nature does  it really matter if we know about the  nature of light  the chemical components of DNA or the  positions of the stars and planets  should we not rather attend to the  meaning and purpose of human life  centuries before petrarch Aristotle  answered this last question there were  thinkers in his day like semana DS who  said that because we are human we must  think of human things because we are  mortal we must think of mortal things  Aristotle rejected such limits on human  thought we must he said so far as we can  make ourselves immortal and strain every  nerve to live in accordance with the  best thing in us even if those  activities in keeping with that divine  in us our reason only take up a small  part of our lives much more do they in  power and worth surpass everything what  does the best of humanism tell us about  the human being anyway that we are  animals capable of reasoning an  articulate speech that human beings  desire to know about things that are not  human that go beyond what is necessary  for human survival now Aristotle says  that all human beings by nature desire  to know in the opening of his  metaphysics where he immediately points  his students to a science that deals  with divine objects so aren't we just  now pitting the humanists attending to  history and poetry against the  Philosopher's devoted to metaphysics and  epistemology where does this lead the  students of the natural world whatever  his failings as a scientist Aristotle  praised those even his philosophical  opponents who dwell in intimate  association with nature and it's  